docstring": "Dummy function to make sure docstrings don't get rendered as text", "start_line": 80, "end_line": 82, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "========================\nTitle of Example\n========================\n\nThis example <verb> <active tense> <does something>.\n\nThe example uses <packages> to <do something> and <other package> to <do other\nthing>. Include links to referenced packages like this: `astropy.io.fits` to\nshow the astropy.io.fits or like this `~astropy.io.fits`to show just 'fits'\n\n\n*By: <names>*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/coordinates/plot_sgr-coordinate-frame.py", "file_hash": "fbd28ab7bdfa9a10d297d8800754668ef058e7c6f2ae907dcf81cdd82e53ae90", "imports": [{"module": "matplotlib.pyplot", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "plt"}, {"module": "numpy", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "np"}, {"module": "astropy.visualization", "names": ["astropy_mpl_style"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.coordinates", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "coord"}, {"module": "astropy.units", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "u"}, {"module": "astropy.coordinates", "names": ["frame_transform_graph"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.coordinates.matrix_utilities", "names": ["matrix_transpose", "rotation_matrix"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [{"name": "galactic_to_sgr", "qualified_name": "galactic_to_sgr", "args": [], "decorators": ["frame_transform_graph.transform(coord.StaticMatrixTransform, coord.Galactic, Sagittarius)"], "docstring": "Compute the Galactic spherical to heliocentric Sgr transformation matrix.", "start_line": 146, "end_line": 148, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "sgr_to_galactic", "qualified_name": "sgr_to_galactic", "args": [], "decorators": ["frame_transform_graph.transform(coord.StaticMatrixTransform, Sagittarius, coord.Galactic)"], "docstring": "Compute the heliocentric Sgr to spherical Galactic transformation matrix.", "start_line": 161, "end_line": 163, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}], "classes": [{"name": "Sagittarius", "bases": ["coord.BaseCoordinateFrame"], "methods": [], "docstring": "A Heliocentric spherical coordinate system defined by the orbit\nof the Sagittarius dwarf galaxy, as described in\n    https://ui.adsabs.harvard.edu/abs/2003ApJ...599.1082M\nand further explained in\n    https://www.stsci.edu/~dlaw/Sgr/.\n\nParameters\n----------\nrepresentation : `~astropy.coordinates.Base", "start_line": 67, "end_line": 104}], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "==========================================================\nCreate a new coordinate class (for the Sagittarius stream)\n==========================================================\n\nThis document describes in detail how to subclass and define a custom spherical\ncoordinate frame, as discussed in :ref:`astropy:astropy-coordinates-design` and\nthe docstring for `~astropy.coordinates.BaseCoordinateFrame`. In this example,\nwe will define a coordinate system defined by the plane of orbit of the\nSagittarius Dwarf Galaxy (hereafter Sgr; as defined in Majewski et al. 2003).\nThe Sgr coordinate system is often referred to in terms of two angular\ncoordinates, :math:`\\Lambda,B`.\n\nTo do this, we need to define a subclass of\n`~astropy.coordinates.BaseCoordinateFrame` that knows the names and units of the\ncoordinate system angles in each of the supported representations.  In this case\nwe support `~astropy.coordinates.SphericalRepresentation` with \"Lambda\" and\n\"Beta\". Then we have to define the transformation from this coordinate system to\nsome other built-in system. Here we will use Galactic coordinates, represented\nby the `~astropy.coordinates.Galactic` class.\n\nSee Also\n--------\n* The `gala package <http://gala.adrian.pw/>`_, which defines a number of\n  Astropy coordinate frames for stellar stream coordinate systems.\n* Majewski et al. 2003, \"A Two Micron All Sky Survey View of the Sagittarius\n  Dwarf Galaxy. I. Morphology of the Sagittarius Core and Tidal Arms\",\n  https://arxiv.org/abs/astro-ph/0304198\n* Law & Majewski 2010, \"The Sagittarius Dwarf Galaxy: A Model for Evolution in a\n  Triaxial Milky Way Halo\", https://arxiv.org/abs/1003.1132\n* David Law's Sgr info page https://www.stsci.edu/~dlaw/Sgr/\n\n\n*By: Adrian Price-Whelan, Erik Tollerud*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/coordinates/plot_obs-planning.py", "file_hash": "391c67bc27fecf9ee7f4dd8a6b745b441b4ad3bd61aab7b68a344e2883576f36", "imports": [{"module": "matplotlib.pyplot", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "plt"}, {"module": "numpy", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "np"}, {"module": "astropy.visualization", "names": ["astropy_mpl_style", "quantity_support"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.units", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "u"}, {"module": "astropy.coordinates", "names": ["AltAz", "EarthLocation", "SkyCoord"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.time", "names": ["Time"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.coordinates", "names": ["get_sun"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.coordinates", "names": ["get_body"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "===================================================================\nDetermining and plotting the altitude/azimuth of a celestial object\n===================================================================\n\nThis example demonstrates coordinate transformations and the creation of\nvisibility curves to assist with observing run planning.\n\nIn this example, we make a `~astropy.coordinates.SkyCoord` instance for M33.\nThe altitude-azimuth coordinates are then found using\n`astropy.coordinates.EarthLocation` and `astropy.time.Time` objects.\n\nThis example is meant to demonstrate the capabilities of the\n`astropy.coordinates` package. For more convenient and/or complex observation\nplanning, consider the `astroplan <https://astroplan.readthedocs.org/>`_\npackage.\n\n\n*By: Erik Tollerud, Kelle Cruz*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/coordinates/rv-to-gsr.py", "file_hash": "1913097fc76662574dac525d431f769da2249397c66f51927c119feed8febbc2", "imports": [{"module": "astropy.coordinates", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "coord"}, {"module": "astropy.units", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "u"}], "functions": [{"name": "rv_to_gsr", "qualified_name": "rv_to_gsr", "args": ["c", "v_sun"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Transform a barycentric radial velocity to the Galactic Standard of Rest\n(GSR).\n\nThe input radial velocity must be passed in as a\n\nParameters\n----------\nc : `~astropy.coordinates.BaseCoordinateFrame` subclass instance\n    The radial velocity, associated with a sky coordinates, to be\n    transformed.", "start_line": 72, "end_line": 103, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "================================================================\nConvert a radial velocity to the Galactic Standard of Rest (GSR)\n================================================================\n\nRadial or line-of-sight velocities of sources are often reported in a\nHeliocentric or Solar-system barycentric reference frame. A common\ntransformation incorporates the projection of the Sun's motion along the\nline-of-sight to the target, hence transforming it to a Galactic rest frame\ninstead (sometimes referred to as the Galactic Standard of Rest, GSR). This\ntransformation depends on the assumptions about the orientation of the Galactic\nframe relative to the bary- or Heliocentric frame. It also depends on the\nassumed solar velocity vector. Here we'll demonstrate how to perform this\ntransformation using a sky position and barycentric radial-velocity.\n\n\n*By: Adrian Price-Whelan*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/coordinates/plot_galactocentric-frame.py", "file_hash": "5c9042fdc14da552a85582c43388385474e1c69c247a85c37ac07440ee202bed", "imports": [{"module": "matplotlib.pyplot", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "plt"}, {"module": "numpy", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "np"}, {"module": "astropy.visualization", "names": ["astropy_mpl_style"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.coordinates", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "coord"}, {"module": "astropy.units", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "u"}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "========================================================================\nTransforming positions and velocities to and from a Galactocentric frame\n========================================================================\n\nThis document shows a few examples of how to use and customize the\n`~astropy.coordinates.Galactocentric` frame to transform Heliocentric sky\npositions, distance, proper motions, and radial velocities to a Galactocentric,\nCartesian frame, and the same in reverse.\n\nThe main configurable parameters of the `~astropy.coordinates.Galactocentric`\nframe control the position and velocity of the solar system barycenter within\nthe Galaxy. These are specified by setting the ICRS coordinates of the\nGalactic center, the distance to the Galactic center (the sun-galactic center\nline is always assumed to be the x-axis of the Galactocentric frame), and the\nCartesian 3-velocity of the sun in the Galactocentric frame. We'll first\ndemonstrate how to customize these values, then show how to set the solar motion\ninstead by inputting the proper motion of Sgr A*.\n\nNote that, for brevity, we may refer to the solar system barycenter as just \"the\nsun\" in the examples below.\n\n\n*By: Adrian Price-Whelan*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/io/skip_create-large-fits.py", "file_hash": "27742ef99beef54313e8618d3404661dae9f708f77b78909458fff27957a13ff", "imports": [{"module": "os", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": ""}, {"module": "numpy", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "np"}, {"module": "astropy.io", "names": ["fits"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "==========================================\nCreate a very large FITS file from scratch\n==========================================\n\nThis example demonstrates how to create a large file (larger than will fit in\nmemory) from scratch using `astropy.io.fits`.\n\n\n*By: Erik Bray*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/io/fits-tables.py", "file_hash": "9eba23a2f3259c331c61da0df98f75c5970516fa260d03cbcfba8f52da0afb1a", "imports": [{"module": "astropy.io", "names": ["fits"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.table", "names": ["Table"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.utils.data", "names": ["get_pkg_data_filename"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "=====================================================================\nAccessing data stored as a table in a multi-extension FITS (MEF) file\n=====================================================================\n\nFITS files can often contain large amount of multi-dimensional data and\ntables. This example opens a FITS file with information\nfrom Chandra's HETG-S instrument.\n\nThe example uses `astropy.utils.data` to download multi-extension FITS (MEF)\nfile, `astropy.io.fits` to investigate the header, and\n`astropy.table.Table` to explore the data.\n\n\n*By: Lia Corrales, Adrian Price-Whelan, and Kelle Cruz*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/io/split-jpeg-to-fits.py", "file_hash": "f3dba67320d45736520e92c6dda0de55582acd7ff3ab8c5a2657f7537e0df3c1", "imports": [{"module": "matplotlib.pyplot", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "plt"}, {"module": "numpy", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "np"}, {"module": "PIL", "names": ["Image"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.io", "names": ["fits"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.visualization", "names": ["astropy_mpl_style"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "pprint", "names": ["pprint"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "os", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "=====================================================\nConvert a 3-color image (JPG) to separate FITS images\n=====================================================\n\nThis example opens an RGB JPEG image and writes out each channel as a separate\nFITS (image) file.\n\nThis example uses `pillow <https://python-pillow.org>`_ to read the image,\n`matplotlib.pyplot` to display the image, and `astropy.io.fits` to save FITS files.\n\n\n*By: Erik Bray, Adrian Price-Whelan*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/io/modify-fits-header.py", "file_hash": "0b0d3551bf49108fdc8ad2bd73bcf056f03e2d5ed4b7becc21c05b9be78a849a", "imports": [{"module": "astropy.io", "names": ["fits"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.utils.data", "names": ["get_pkg_data_filename"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "==================\nEdit a FITS header\n==================\n\nThis example describes how to edit a value in a FITS header\nusing `astropy.io.fits`.\n\n\n*By: Adrian Price-Whelan*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/io/plot_fits-image.py", "file_hash": "f0a9c8c2045828483baf41056c9d2a713b4276104f34192366feeb452f71edc8", "imports": [{"module": "matplotlib.pyplot", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "plt"}, {"module": "astropy.visualization", "names": ["astropy_mpl_style"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.io", "names": ["fits"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.utils.data", "names": ["get_pkg_data_filename"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "=======================================\nRead and plot an image from a FITS file\n=======================================\n\nThis example opens an image stored in a FITS file and displays it to the screen.\n\nThis example uses `astropy.utils.data` to download the file, `astropy.io.fits` to open\nthe file, and `matplotlib.pyplot` to display the image.\n\n\n*By: Lia R. Corrales, Adrian Price-Whelan, Kelle Cruz*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "examples/io/create-mef.py", "file_hash": "51e8664af769c49b9fd938ea4fabc8d8083275e145b9f7c56aaa04d3cb02ba8e", "imports": [{"module": "os", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.io", "names": ["fits"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "=====================================================\nCreate a multi-extension FITS (MEF) file from scratch\n=====================================================\n\nThis example demonstrates how to create a multi-extension FITS (MEF)\nfile from scratch using `astropy.io.fits`.\n\n\n*By: Erik Bray*\n\n*License: BSD*", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "astropy/constants/config.py", "file_hash": "8b7943d33c078bf620dd373fd732b9283f4634bc30a117acd3e0689899cae2b6", "imports": [{"module": "importlib", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "Configures the codata and iaudata used, possibly using user configuration.", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "astropy/constants/codata2010.py", "file_hash": "32971fb0548a3279045d30285b3292f480d1b824e2b18c003b014d626db83ae8", "imports": [{"module": "numpy", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": "np"}, {"module": "constant", "names": ["Constant", "EMConstant"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [{"name": "__new__", "qualified_name": "__new__", "args": ["cls", "abbrev", "name", "value", "unit", "uncertainty", "reference", "system"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 19, "end_line": 31, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}], "classes": [{"name": "CODATA2010", "bases": ["Constant"], "methods": ["__new__"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 14, "end_line": 31}, {"name": "EMCODATA2010", "bases": ["CODATA2010", "EMConstant"], "methods": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 34, "end_line": 35}], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "Astronomical and physics constants in SI units.  See :mod:`astropy.constants`\nfor a complete listing of constants defined in Astropy.", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "astropy/constants/__init__.py", "file_hash": "fb6138ebebd4e766de14a4c1faf228ada96a63c1e040dfff6177563d45798353", "imports": [{"module": "warnings", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy.utils", "names": ["find_current_module"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "astropy", "names": ["units"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "", "names": ["cgs", "si"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "", "names": ["utils"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "config", "names": ["codata", "iaudata"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "constant", "names": ["Constant", "EMConstant"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "Contains astronomical and physical constants for use in Astropy or other\nplaces.\n\nA typical use case might be::\n\n    >>> from astropy.constants import c, m_e\n    >>> # ... define the mass of something you want the rest energy of as m ...\n    >>> m = m_e\n    >>> E = m * c**2\n    >>> E.to('MeV')  # doctest: +FLOAT_CMP\n    <Quantity 0.510998927603161 MeV>", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "astropy/constants/utils.py", "file_hash": "a7d672f77e37011b146d6780a5653789fe4ec24bbb16d5e1816bd462de16f48d", "imports": [{"module": "itertools", "names": [], "is_from": false, "alias": ""}, {"module": "constant", "names": ["Constant"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [{"name": "_get_c", "qualified_name": "_get_c", "args": ["codata", "iaudata", "module", "not_in_module_only"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Generator to return a Constant object.\n\nParameters\n----------\ncodata, iaudata : obj\n    Modules containing CODATA and IAU constants of interest.\n\nmodule : obj\n    Namespace module of interest.\n\nnot_in_module_only : bool\n    If ``True``, ignore constants that are already in the\n    namespace of ``mod", "start_line": 8, "end_line": 38, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_set_c", "qualified_name": "_set_c", "args": ["codata", "iaudata", "module", "not_in_module_only", "doclines", "set_class"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Set constants in a given module namespace.\n\nParameters\n----------\ncodata, iaudata : obj\n    Modules containing CODATA and IAU constants of interest.\n\nmodule : obj\n    Namespace module to modify with the given ``codata`` and ``iaudata``.\n\nnot_in_module_only : bool\n    If ``True``, constants that are ", "start_line": 41, "end_line": 87, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "Utility functions for ``constants`` sub-package.", "parse_error": ""}, {"file_path": "astropy/constants/astropyconst13.py", "file_hash": "b0178bb8388a3015d0974ce61b2fe5bf201adea6495584ade50f10ab30540c78", "imports": [{"module": "astropy.utils", "names": ["find_current_module"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "", "names": ["codata2010", "iau2012"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}, {"module": "", "names": ["utils"], "is_from": true, "alias": ""}], "functions": [], "classes": [], "calls": [], "module_docstring": "Astronomical and physics constants for Astropy v1.3 and earlier.\nSee :mod:`astropy.constants` f