 time format.\n\nThis represents the number of days since midnight on November 17, 1858.\nFor example, 51544.0 in MJD is midnight on January 1, 2000.", "start_line": 563, "end_line": 584}, {"name": "TimeDecimalYear", "bases": ["TimeNumeric"], "methods": ["_check_val_type", "set_jds", "to_value"], "docstring": "Time as a decimal year, with integer values corresponding to midnight\nof the first day of each year.\n\nFor example 2000.5 corresponds to the ISO time '2000-07-02 00:00:00'.\n\nSince for this format the length of the year varies between 365 and\n366 days, it is not possible to use Quantity input, in whic", "start_line": 598, "end_line": 669}, {"name": "TimeFromEpoch", "bases": ["TimeNumeric"], "methods": ["_epoch", "epoch", "set_jds", "to_value", "_default_scale"], "docstring": "Base class for times that represent the interval from a particular\nepoch as a numerical multiple of a unit time interval (e.g. seconds\nor days).", "start_line": 672, "end_line": 780}, {"name": "TimeUnix", "bases": ["TimeFromEpoch"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Unix time (UTC): seconds from 1970-01-01 00:00:00 UTC, ignoring leap seconds.\n\nFor example, 946684800.0 in Unix time is midnight on January 1, 2000.\n\nNOTE: this quantity is not exactly unix time and differs from the strict\nPOSIX definition by up to 1 second on days with a leap second.  POSIX\nunix ti", "start_line": 783, "end_line": 801}, {"name": "TimeUnixTai", "bases": ["TimeUnix"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Unix time (TAI): SI seconds elapsed since 1970-01-01 00:00:00 TAI (see caveats).\n\nThis will generally differ from standard (UTC) Unix time by the cumulative\nintegral number of leap seconds introduced into UTC since 1972-01-01 UTC\nplus the initial offset of 10 seconds at that date.\n\nThis convention m", "start_line": 804, "end_line": 853}, {"name": "TimeCxcSec", "bases": ["TimeFromEpoch"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Chandra X-ray Center seconds from 1998-01-01 00:00:00 TT.\nFor example, 63072064.184 is midnight on January 1, 2000.", "start_line": 856, "end_line": 867}, {"name": "TimeGPS", "bases": ["TimeFromEpoch"], "methods": [], "docstring": "GPS time: seconds from 1980-01-06 00:00:00 UTC\nFor example, 630720013.0 is midnight on January 1, 2000.\n\nNotes\n-----\nThis implementation is strictly a representation of the number of seconds\n(including leap seconds) since midnight UTC on 1980-01-06.  GPS can also be\nconsidered as a time scale which ", "start_line": 870, "end_line": 890}, {"name": "TimePlotDate", "bases": ["TimeFromEpoch"], "methods": ["epoch"], "docstring": "Matplotlib `~matplotlib.pyplot.plot_date` input:\n1 + number of days from 0001-01-01 00:00:00 UTC.\n\nThis can be used directly in the matplotlib `~matplotlib.pyplot.plot_date`\nfunction::\n\n  >>> import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n  >>> jyear = np.linspace(2000, 2001, 20)\n  >>> t = Time(jyear, format='jyea", "start_line": 893, "end_line": 940}, {"name": "TimeStardate", "bases": ["TimeFromEpoch"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Stardate: date units from 2318-07-05 12:00:00 UTC.\nFor example, stardate 41153.7 is 00:52 on April 30, 2363.\nSee http://trekguide.com/Stardates.htm#TNG for calculations and reference points.", "start_line": 943, "end_line": 955}, {"name": "TimeUnique", "bases": ["TimeFormat"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Base class for time formats that can uniquely create a time object\nwithout requiring an explicit format specifier.  This class does\nnothing but provide inheritance to identify a class as unique.", "start_line": 958, "end_line": 963}, {"name": "TimeAstropyTime", "bases": ["TimeUnique"], "methods": ["__new__"], "docstring": "Instantiate date from an Astropy Time object (or list thereof).\n\nThis is purely for instantiating from a Time object.  The output\nformat is the same as the first time instance.", "start_line": 966, "end_line": 1033}, {"name": "TimeDatetime", "bases": ["TimeUnique"], "methods": ["_check_val_type", "set_jds", "to_value"], "docstring": "Represent date as Python standard library `~datetime.datetime` object.\n\nExample::\n\n  >>> from astropy.time import Time\n  >>> from datetime import datetime\n  >>> t = Time(datetime(2000, 1, 2, 12, 0, 0), scale='utc')\n  >>> t.iso\n  '2000-01-02 12:00:00.000'\n  >>> t.tt.datetime\n  datetime.datetime(2000,", "start_line": 1036, "end_line": 1184}, {"name": "TimeYMDHMS", "bases": ["TimeUnique"], "methods": ["_check_val_type", "set_jds", "value"], "docstring": "ymdhms: A Time format to represent Time as year, month, day, hour,\nminute, second (thus the name ymdhms).\n\nAcceptable inputs must have keys or column names in the \"YMDHMS\" set of\n``year``, ``month``, ``day`` ``hour``, ``minute``, ``second``:\n\n- Dict with keys in the YMDHMS set\n- NumPy structured arr", "start_line": 1187, "end_line": 1334}, {"name": "TimezoneInfo", "bases": ["datetime.tzinfo"], "methods": ["__init__", "utcoffset", "tzname", "dst"], "docstring": "Subclass of the `~datetime.tzinfo` object, used in the\nto_datetime method to specify timezones.\n\nIt may be safer in most cases to use a timezone database package like\npytz rather than defining your own timezones - this class is mainly\na workaround for users without pytz.", "start_line": 1337, "end_line": 1386}, {"name": "TimeString", "bases": ["TimeUnique"], "methods": ["__init_subclass__", "_check_val_type", "parse_string", "set_jds", "get_jds_python", "get_jds_fast", "str_kwargs", "format_string", "value"], "docstring": "Base class for string-like time representations.\n\nThis class assumes that anything following the last decimal point to the\nright is a fraction of a second.\n\n**Fast C-based parser**\n\nTime format classes can take advantage of a fast C-based parser if the times\nare represented as fixed-format strings w", "start_line": 1389, "end_line": 1664}, {"name": "TimeISO", "bases": ["TimeString"], "methods": ["parse_string"], "docstring": "ISO 8601 compliant date-time format \"YYYY-MM-DD HH:MM:SS.sss...\".\nFor example, 2000-01-01 00:00:00.000 is midnight on January 1, 2000.\n\nThe allowed subformats are:\n\n- 'date_hms': date + hours, mins, secs (and optional fractional secs)\n- 'date_hm': date + hours, mins\n- 'date': date", "start_line": 1667, "end_line": 1719}, {"name": "TimeISOT", "bases": ["TimeISO"], "methods": [], "docstring": "ISO 8601 compliant date-time format \"YYYY-MM-DDTHH:MM:SS.sss...\".\nThis is the same as TimeISO except for a \"T\" instead of space between\nthe date and time.\nFor example, 2000-01-01T00:00:00.000 is midnight on January 1, 2000.\n\nThe allowed subformats are:\n\n- 'date_hms': date + hours, mins, secs (and op", "start_line": 1722, "end_line": 1760}, {"name": "TimeYearDayTime", "bases": ["TimeISO"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Year, day-of-year and time as \"YYYY:DOY:HH:MM:SS.sss...\".\nThe day-of-year (DOY) goes from 001 to 365 (366 in leap years).\nFor example, 2000:001:00:00:00.000 is midnight on January 1, 2000.\n\nThe allowed subformats are:\n\n- 'date_hms': date + hours, mins, secs (and optional fractional secs)\n- 'date_hm'", "start_line": 1763, "end_line": 1808}, {"name": "TimeDatetime64", "bases": ["TimeISOT"], "methods": ["_check_val_type", "set_jds", "value"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 1811, "end_line": 1858}, {"name": "TimeFITS", "bases": ["TimeString"], "methods": ["parse_string", "value"], "docstring": "FITS format: \"[\u00b1Y]YYYY-MM-DD[THH:MM:SS[.sss]]\".\n\nISOT but can give signed five-digit year (mostly for negative years);\n\nThe allowed subformats are:\n\n- 'date_hms': date + hours, mins, secs (and optional fractional secs)\n- 'date': date\n- 'longdate_hms': as 'date_hms', but with signed 5-digit year\n- 'l", "start_line": 1861, "end_line": 1973}, {"name": "TimeEpochDate", "bases": ["TimeNumeric"], "methods": ["set_jds", "to_value"], "docstring": "Base class for support of Besselian and Julian epoch dates.", "start_line": 1976, "end_line": 1994}, {"name": "TimeBesselianEpoch", "bases": ["TimeEpochDate"], "methods": ["_check_val_type"], "docstring": "Besselian Epoch year as value(s) like 1950.0.\n\nSince for this format the length of the year varies, input needs to\nbe floating point; it is not possible to use Quantity input, for\nwhich a year always equals 365.25 days.", "start_line": 1997, "end_line": 2012}, {"name": "TimeJulianEpoch", "bases": ["TimeEpochDate"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Julian Epoch year as value(s) like 2000.0.", "start_line": 2015, "end_line": 2021}, {"name": "TimeEpochDateString", "bases": ["TimeString"], "methods": ["set_jds", "value"], "docstring": "Base class to support string Besselian and Julian epoch dates\nsuch as 'B1950.0' or 'J2000.0' respectively.", "start_line": 2024, "end_line": 2065}, {"name": "TimeBesselianEpochString", "bases": ["TimeEpochDateString"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Besselian Epoch year as string value(s) like 'B1950.0'.", "start_line": 2068, "end_line": 2074}, {"name": "TimeJulianEpochString", "bases": ["TimeEpochDateString"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Julian Epoch year as string value(s) like 'J2000.0'.", "start_line": 2077, "end_line": 2083}, {"name": "TimeDeltaFormat", "bases": ["TimeFormat"], "methods": ["_check_scale"], "docstring": "Base class for time delta representations.", "start_line": 2086, "end_line": 2100}, {"name": "TimeDeltaNumeric", "bases": ["TimeDeltaFormat", "TimeNumeric"], "methods": ["set_jds", "to_value"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 2103, "end_line": 2116}, {"name": "TimeDeltaSec", "bases": ["TimeDeltaNumeric"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Time delta in SI seconds.", "start_line": 2119, "end_line": 2123}, {"name": "TimeDeltaJD", "bases": ["TimeDeltaNumeric"], "methods": [], "docstring": "Time delta in Julian days (86400 SI seconds).", "start_line": 2126, "end_line": 2130}, {"name": "TimeDeltaDatetime", "bases": ["TimeDeltaFormat", "TimeUnique"], "methods": ["_check_val_type", "set_jds", "value"], "docstring": "Time delta in datetime.timedelta.", "start_line": 2133, "end_line": 2176}], "calls": [], "module_docstring": ""