ct to the large array, tuples of slices are returned which can be\nused to extract, add or subtract the small array at the given\nposition. This function takes care o", "start_line": 40, "end_line": 155, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "extract_array", "qualified_name": "extract_array", "args": ["array_large", "shape", "position", "mode", "fill_value", "return_position"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Extract a smaller array of the given shape and position from a\nlarger array.\n\nParameters\n----------\narray_large : ndarray\n    The array from which to extract the small array.\nshape : int or tuple thereof\n    The shape of the extracted array (for 1D arrays, this can be an\n    `int`).  See the ``mode`", "start_line": 158, "end_line": 267, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "add_array", "qualified_name": "add_array", "args": ["array_large", "array_small", "position"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Add a smaller array at a given position in a larger array.\n\nParameters\n----------\narray_large : ndarray\n    Large array.\narray_small : ndarray\n    Small array to add. Can be equal to ``array_large`` in size in a given\n    dimension, but not larger.\nposition : tuple\n    Position of the small array's ", "start_line": 270, "end_line": 322, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "subpixel_indices", "qualified_name": "subpixel_indices", "args": ["position", "subsampling"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert decimal points to indices, given a subsampling factor.\n\nThis discards the integer part of the position and uses only the decimal\nplace, and converts this to a subpixel position depending on the\nsubsampling specified. The center of a pixel corresponds to an integer\nposition.\n\nParameters\n-----", "start_line": 325, "end_line": 364, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__init__", "qualified_name": "__init__", "args": ["self", "data", "position", "size", "wcs", "mode", "fill_value", "copy"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 546, "end_line": 654, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "to_original_position", "qualified_name": "to_original_position", "args": ["self", "cutout_position"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert an ``(x, y)`` position in the cutout array to the original\n``(x, y)`` position in the original large array.\n\nParameters\n----------\ncutout_position : tuple\n    The ``(x, y)`` pixel position in the cutout array.\n\nReturns\n-------\noriginal_position : tuple\n    The corresponding ``(x, y)`` pixel ", "start_line": 656, "end_line": 672, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "to_cutout_position", "qualified_name": "to_cutout_position", "args": ["self", "original_position"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert an ``(x, y)`` position in the original large array to\nthe ``(x, y)`` position in the cutout array.\n\nParameters\n----------\noriginal_position : tuple\n    The ``(x, y)`` pixel position in the original large array.\n\nReturns\n-------\ncutout_position : tuple\n    The corresponding ``(x, y)`` pixel p", "start_line": 674, "end_line": 690, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "plot_on_original", "qualified_name": "plot_on_original", "args": ["self", "ax", "fill"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Plot the cutout region on a matplotlib Axes instance.\n\nParameters\n----------\nax : `matplotlib.axes.Axes` instance, optional\n    If `None`, then the current `matplotlib.axes.Axes` instance\n    is used.\n\nfill : bool, optional\n    Set whether to fill the cutout patch.  The default is\n    `False`.\n\nkwar", "start_line": 692, "end_line": 729, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_calc_center", "qualified_name": "_calc_center", "args": ["slices"], "decorators": ["staticmethod"], "docstring": "Calculate the center position.  The center position will be\nfractional for even-sized arrays.  For ``mode='partial'``, the\ncentral position is calculated for the valid (non-filled) cutout\nvalues.", "start_line": 732, "end_line": 739, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_calc_bbox", "qualified_name": "_calc_bbox", "args": ["slices"], "decorators": ["staticmethod"], "docstring": "Calculate a minimal bounding box in the form ``((ymin, ymax),\n(xmin, xmax))``.  Note these are pixel locations, not slice\nindices.  For ``mode='partial'``, the bounding box indices are\nfor the valid (non-filled) cutout values.", "start_line": 742, "end_line": 753, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "origin_original", "qualified_name": "origin_original", "args": ["self"], "decorators": ["lazyproperty"], "docstring": "The ``(x, y)`` index of the origin pixel of the cutout with\nrespect to the original array.  For ``mode='partial'``, the\norigin pixel is calculated for the valid (non-filled) cutout\nvalues.", "start_line": 756, "end_line": 763, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "origin_cutout", "qualified_name": "origin_cutout", "args": ["self"], "decorators": ["lazyproperty"], "docstring": "The ``(x, y)`` index of the origin pixel of the cutout with\nrespect to the cutout array.  For ``mode='partial'``, the origin\npixel is calculated for the valid (non-filled) cutout values.", "start_line": 766, "end_line": 772, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_round", "qualified_name": "_round", "args": ["a"], "decorators": ["staticmethod"], "docstring": "Round the input to the nearest integer.\n\nIf two integers are equally close, the value is rounded up.\nNote that this is different from `np.round`, which rounds to the\nnearest even number.", "start_line": 775, "end_line": 783, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "position_original", "qualified_name": "position_original", "args": ["self"], "decorators": ["lazyproperty"], "docstring": "The ``(x, y)`` position index (rounded to the nearest pixel) in\nthe original array.", "start_line": 786, "end_line": 794, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "p