lf", "names"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Remove several columns from the table.\n\nParameters\n----------\nnames : str or iterable of str\n    Names of the columns to remove\n\nExamples\n--------\nCreate a table with three columns 'a', 'b' and 'c'::\n\n    >>> t = Table([[1, 2, 3], [0.1, 0.2, 0.3], ['x', 'y', 'z']],\n    ...     names=('a', 'b', 'c'))", "start_line": 2868, "end_line": 2915, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_convert_string_dtype", "qualified_name": "_convert_string_dtype", "args": ["self", "in_kind", "out_kind", "encode_decode_func"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert string-like columns to/from bytestring and unicode (internal only).\n\nParameters\n----------\nin_kind : str\n    Input dtype.kind\nout_kind : str\n    Output dtype.kind", "start_line": 2917, "end_line": 2946, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "convert_bytestring_to_unicode", "qualified_name": "convert_bytestring_to_unicode", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert bytestring columns (dtype.kind='S') to unicode (dtype.kind='U')\nusing UTF-8 encoding.\n\nInternally this changes string columns to represent each character\nin the string with a 4-byte UCS-4 equivalent, so it is inefficient\nfor memory but allows scripts to manipulate string arrays with\nnatural ", "start_line": 2948, "end_line": 2958, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "convert_unicode_to_bytestring", "qualified_name": "convert_unicode_to_bytestring", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert unicode columns (dtype.kind='U') to bytestring (dtype.kind='S')\nusing UTF-8 encoding.\n\nWhen exporting a unicode string array to a file, it may be desirable\nto encode unicode columns as bytestrings.", "start_line": 2960, "end_line": 2968, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "keep_columns", "qualified_name": "keep_columns", "args": ["self", "names"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Keep only the columns specified (remove the others).\n\nParameters\n----------\nnames : str or iterable of str\n    The columns to keep. All other columns will be removed.\n\nExamples\n--------\nCreate a table with three columns 'a', 'b' and 'c'::\n\n    >>> t = Table([[1, 2, 3],[0.1, 0.2, 0.3],['x', 'y', 'z']", "start_line": 2970, "end_line": 3017, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "rename_column", "qualified_name": "rename_column", "args": ["self", "name", "new_name"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Rename a column.\n\nThis can also be done directly by setting the ``name`` attribute\nof the ``info`` property of the column::\n\n  table[name].info.name = new_name\n\nParameters\n----------\nname : str\n    The current name of the column.\nnew_name : str\n    The new name for the column\n\nExamples\n--------\nCrea", "start_line": 3019, "end_line": 3058, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "rename_columns", "qualified_name": "rename_columns", "args": ["self", "names", "new_names"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Rename multiple columns.\n\nParameters\n----------\nnames : list, tuple\n    A list or tuple of existing column names.\nnew_names : list, tuple\n    A list or tuple of new column names.\n\nExamples\n--------\nCreate a table with three columns 'a', 'b', 'c'::\n\n    >>> t = Table([[1,2],[3,4],[5,6]], names=('a','", "start_line": 3060, "end_line": 3107, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_set_row", "qualified_name": "_set_row", "args": ["self", "idx", "colnames", "vals"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3109, "end_line": 3130, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "add_row", "qualified_name": "add_row", "args": ["self", "vals", "mask"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Add a new row to the end of the table.\n\nThe ``vals`` argument can be:\n\nsequence (e.g. tuple or list)\n    Column values in the same order as table columns.\nmapping (e.g. dict)\n    Keys corresponding to column names.  Missing values will be\n    filled with np.zeros for the column dtype.\n`None`\n    All", "start_line": 3132, "end_line": 3183, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "insert_row", "qualified_name": "insert_row", "args": ["self", "index", "vals", "mask"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Add a new row before the given ``index`` position in the table.\n\nThe ``vals`` argument can be:\n\nsequence (e.g. tuple or list)\n    Column values in the same order as table columns.\nmapping (e.g. dict)\n    Keys corresponding to column names.  Missing values will be\n    filled with np.zeros for the col", "start_line": 3185, "end_line": 3328, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_replace_cols", "qualified_name": "_replace_cols", "args": ["self", "columns"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3330, "end_line": 3337, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "update", "qualified_name": "update", "args": ["self", "other", "copy"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Perform a dictionary-style update and merge metadata.\n\nThe argument ``other`` must be a |Table|, or something that can be used\nto initialize a table. Columns from (possibly converted) ``other`` are\nadded to this table. In case of matching column names the column from\nthis table is replaced with the ", "start_line": 3339, "end_line": 3401, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "argsort", "qualified_name": "argsort", "args": ["self", "keys", "kind", "reverse"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Return the indices which would sort the table according to one or\nmore key columns.  This simply calls the `numpy.argsort` function on\nthe table with the ``order`` parameter set to ``keys``.\n\nParameters\n----------\nkeys : str or list of str\n    The column name(s) to order the table by\nkind : {'quicks", "start_line": 3403, "end_line": 3458, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "sort", "qualified_name": "sort", "args": ["self", "keys"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Sort the table according to one or more keys. This operates\non the existing table and does not return a new table.\n\nParameters\n----------\nkeys : str or list of str\n    The key(s) to order the table by. If None, use the\n    primary index of the Table.\nkind : {'quicksort', 'mergesort', 'heapsort', 'st", "start_line": 3460, "end_line": 3534, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "reverse", "qualified_name": "reverse", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Reverse the row order of table rows.  The table is reversed\nin place and there are no function arguments.\n\nExamples\n--------\nCreate a table with three columns::\n\n    >>> t = Table([['Max', 'Jo', 'John'], ['Miller','Miller','Jackson'],\n    ...         [12,15,18]], names=('firstname','name','tel'))\n  ", "start_line": 3536, "end_line": 3578, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "round", "qualified_name": "round", "args": ["self", "decimals"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Round numeric columns in-place to the specified number of decimals.\nNon-numeric columns will be ignored.\n\nExamples\n--------\nCreate three columns with different types:\n\n    >>> t = Table([[1, 4, 5], [-25.55, 12.123, 85],\n    ...     ['a', 'b', 'c']], names=('a', 'b', 'c'))\n    >>> print(t)\n     a    ", "start_line": 3580, "end_line": 3642, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "copy", "qualified_name": "copy", "args": ["self", "copy_data"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Return a copy of the table.\n\nParameters\n----------\ncopy_data : bool\n    If `True` (the default), copy the underlying data array.\n    Otherwise, use the same data array. The ``meta`` is always\n    deepcopied regardless of the value for ``copy_data``.", "start_line": 3644, "end_line": 3662, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__deepcopy__", "qualified_name": "__deepcopy__", "args": ["self", "memo"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3664, "end_line": 3665, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__copy__", "qualified_name": "__copy__", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3667, "end_line": 3668, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__lt__", "qualified_name": "__lt__", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3670, "end_line": 3671, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__gt__", "qualified_name": "__gt__", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3673, "end_line": 3674, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__le__", "qualified_name": "__le__", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3676, "end_line": 3677, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__ge__", "qualified_name": "__ge__", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3679, "end_line": 3680, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__eq__", "qualified_name": "__eq__", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3682, "end_line": 3683, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__ne__", "qualified_name": "__ne__", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3685, "end_line": 3686, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_rows_equal", "qualified_name": "_rows_equal", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Row-wise comparison of table with any other object.\n\nThis is actual implementation for __eq__.\n\nReturns a 1-D boolean numpy array showing result of row-wise comparison.\nThis is the same as the ``==`` comparison for tables.\n\nParameters\n----------\nother : Table or DataFrame or ndarray\n     An object t", "start_line": 3688, "end_line": 3732, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "values_equal", "qualified_name": "values_equal", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Element-wise comparison of table with another table, list, or scalar.\n\nReturns a ``Table`` with the same columns containing boolean values\nshowing result of comparison.\n\nParameters\n----------\nother : table-like object or list or scalar\n     Object to compare with table\n\nExamples\n--------\nCompare one", "start_line": 3734, "end_line": 3814, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "groups", "qualified_name": "groups", "args": ["self"], "decorators": ["property"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 3817, "end_line": 3820, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "group_by", "qualified_name": "group_by", "args": ["self", "keys"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Group this table by the specified ``keys``.\n\nThis effectively splits the table into groups which correspond to unique\nvalues of the ``keys`` grouping object.  The output is a new\n`~astropy.table.TableGroups` which contains a copy of this table but\nsorted by row according to ``keys``.\n\nThe ``keys`` i", "start_line": 3822, "end_line": 3847, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "to_pandas", "qualified_name": "to_pandas", "args": ["self", "index", "use_nullable_int"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Return a :class:`pandas.DataFrame` instance.\n\nThe index of the created DataFrame is controlled by the ``index``\nargument.  For ``index=True`` or the default ``None``, an index will be\nspecified for the DataFrame if there is a primary key index on the\nTable *and* if it corresponds to a single column.", "start_line": 3849, "end_line": 4033, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "from_pandas", "qualified_name": "from_pandas", "args": ["cls", "dataframe", "index", "units"], "decorators": ["classmethod"], "docstring": "Create a `~astropy.table.Table` from a :class:`pandas.DataFrame` instance.\n\nIn addition to converting generic numeric or string columns, this supports\nconversion of pandas Date and Time delta columns to `~astropy.time.Time`\nand `~astropy.time.TimeDelta` columns, respectively.\n\nParameters\n----------\n", "start_line": 4036, "end_line": 4170, "is_as