decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 76, "end_line": 107, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "new_like", "qualified_name": "new_like", "args": ["self", "skycoords", "length", "metadata_conflicts", "name"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Return a new SkyCoord instance which is consistent with the input\nSkyCoord objects ``skycoords`` and has ``length`` rows.  Being\n\"consistent\" is defined as being able to set an item from one to each of\nthe rest without any exception being raised.\n\nThis is intended for creating a new SkyCoord instanc", "start_line": 109, "end_line": 165, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__init__", "qualified_name": "__init__", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 291, "end_line": 359, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "frame", "qualified_name": "frame", "args": ["self"], "decorators": ["property"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 362, "end_line": 363, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "representation_type", "qualified_name": "representation_type", "args": ["self"], "decorators": ["property"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 366, "end_line": 367, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "representation_type", "qualified_name": "representation_type", "args": ["self", "value"], "decorators": ["representation_type.setter"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 370, "end_line": 371, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "shape", "qualified_name": "shape", "args": ["self"], "decorators": ["property"], "docstring": "", "start_line": 374, "end_line": 375, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__eq__", "qualified_name": "__eq__", "args": ["self", "value"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Equality operator for SkyCoord.\n\nThis implements strict equality and requires that the frames are\nequivalent, extra frame attributes are equivalent, and that the\nrepresentation data are exactly equal.", "start_line": 377, "end_line": 403, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__ne__", "qualified_name": "__ne__", "args": ["self", "value"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 405, "end_line": 406, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_apply", "qualified_name": "_apply", "args": ["self", "method"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Create a new instance, applying a method to the underlying data.\n\nIn typical usage, the method is any of the shape-changing methods for\n`~numpy.ndarray` (``reshape``, ``swapaxes``, etc.), as well as those\npicking particular elements (``__getitem__``, ``take``, etc.), which\nare all defined in `~astro", "start_line": 408, "end_line": 464, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__setitem__", "qualified_name": "__setitem__", "args": ["self", "item", "value"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Implement self[item] = value for SkyCoord.\n\nThe right hand ``value`` must be strictly consistent with self:\n- Identical class\n- Equivalent frames\n- Identical representation_types\n- Identical representation differentials keys\n- Identical frame attributes\n- Identical \"extra\" frame attributes (e.g. obs", "start_line": 466, "end_line": 497, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "insert", "qualified_name": "insert", "args": ["self", "obj", "values", "axis"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Insert coordinate values before the given indices in the object and\nreturn a new Frame object.\n\nThe values to be inserted must conform to the rules for in-place setting\nof |SkyCoord| objects.\n\nThe API signature matches the ``np.insert`` API, but is more limited.\nThe specification of insert index ``o", "start_line": 499, "end_line": 566, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "is_transformable_to", "qualified_name": "is_transformable_to", "args": ["self", "new_frame"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Determines if this coordinate frame can be transformed to another\ngiven frame.\n\nParameters\n----------\nnew_frame : frame class, frame object, or str\n    The proposed frame to transform into.\n\nReturns\n-------\ntransformable : bool or str\n    `True` if this can be transformed to ``new_frame``, `False` i", "start_line": 568, "end_line": 604, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "transform_to", "qualified_name": "transform_to", "args": ["self", "frame", "merge_attributes"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Transform this coordinate to a new frame.\n\nThe precise frame transformed to depends on ``merge_attributes``.\nIf `False`, the destination frame is used exactly as passed in.\nBut this is often not quite what one wants.  E.g., suppose one wants to\ntransform an ICRS coordinate that has an obstime attrib", "start_line": 606, "end_line": 706, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "apply_space_motion", "qualified_name": "apply_space_motion", "args": ["self", "new_obstime", "dt"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Compute the position to a new time using the velocities.\n\nCompute the position of the source represented by this coordinate object\nto a new time using the velocities stored in this object and assuming\nlinear space motion (including relativistic corrections). This is\nsometimes referred to as an \"epoc", "start_line": 708, "end_line": 848, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "_is_name", "qualified_name": "_is_name", "args": ["self", "string"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Returns whether a string is one of the aliases for the frame.", "start_line": 850, "end_line": 856, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__getattr__", "qualified_name": "__getattr__", "args": ["self", "attr"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Overrides getattr to return coordinates that this can be transformed\nto, based on the alias attr in the primary transform graph.", "start_line": 858, "end_line": 887, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__setattr__", "qualified_name": "__setattr__", "args": ["self", "attr", "val"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 889, "end_line": 914, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__delattr__", "qualified_name": "__delattr__", "args": ["self", "attr"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 916, "end_line": 939, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__dir__", "qualified_name": "__dir__", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Original dir() behavior, plus frame attributes and transforms.\n\nThis dir includes:\n- All attributes of the SkyCoord class\n- Coordinate transforms available by aliases\n- Attribute / methods of the underlying self.frame objects", "start_line": 941, "end_line": 965, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "__repr__", "qualified_name": "__repr__", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "", "start_line": 967, "end_line": 978, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "to_string", "qualified_name": "to_string", "args": ["self", "style"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "A string representation of the coordinates.\n\nThe default styles definitions are::\n\n  'decimal': 'lat': {'decimal': True, 'unit': \"deg\"}\n             'lon': {'decimal': True, 'unit': \"deg\"}\n  'dms': 'lat': {'unit': \"deg\"}\n         'lon': {'unit': \"deg\"}\n  'hmsdms': 'lat': {'alwayssign': True, 'pad': ", "start_line": 980, "end_line": 1047, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "to_table", "qualified_name": "to_table", "args": ["self"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert this |SkyCoord| to a |QTable|.\n\nAny attributes that have the same length as the |SkyCoord| will be\nconverted to columns of the |QTable|. All other attributes will be\nrecorded as metadata.\n\nReturns\n-------\n`~astropy.table.QTable`\n    A |QTable| containing the data of this |SkyCoord|.\n\nExample", "start_line": 1049, "end_line": 1089, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "is_equivalent_frame", "qualified_name": "is_equivalent_frame", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Checks if this object's frame as the same as that of the ``other``\nobject.\n\nTo be the same frame, two objects must be the same frame class and have\nthe same frame attributes. For two |SkyCoord| objects, *all* of the\nframe attributes have to match, not just those relevant for the object's\nframe.\n\nPar", "start_line": 1091, "end_line": 1133, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "separation", "qualified_name": "separation", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Computes on-sky separation between this coordinate and another.\n\n.. note::\n\n    If the ``other`` coordinate object is in a different frame, it is\n    first transformed to the frame of this object. This can lead to\n    unintuitive behavior if not accounted for. Particularly of note is\n    that ``self", "start_line": 1136, "end_line": 1191, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "separation_3d", "qualified_name": "separation_3d", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Computes three dimensional separation between this coordinate\nand another.\n\nFor more on how to use this (and related) functionality, see the\nexamples in :doc:`astropy:/coordinates/matchsep`.\n\nParameters\n----------\nother : `~astropy.coordinates.SkyCoord` or `~astropy.coordinates.BaseCoordinateFrame`\n", "start_line": 1193, "end_line": 1240, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "spherical_offsets_to", "qualified_name": "spherical_offsets_to", "args": ["self", "tocoord"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Computes angular offsets to go *from* this coordinate *to* another.\n\nParameters\n----------\ntocoord : `~astropy.coordinates.BaseCoordinateFrame`\n    The coordinate to find the offset to.\n\nReturns\n-------\nlon_offset : `~astropy.coordinates.Angle`\n    The angular offset in the longitude direction. The ", "start_line": 1242, "end_line": 1294, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "spherical_offsets_by", "qualified_name": "spherical_offsets_by", "args": ["self", "d_lon", "d_lat"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Computes the coordinate that is a specified pair of angular offsets away\nfrom this coordinate.\n\nParameters\n----------\nd_lon : angle-like\n    The angular offset in the longitude direction. The definition of\n    \"longitude\" depends on this coordinate's frame (e.g., RA for\n    equatorial coordinates).\n", "start_line": 1296, "end_line": 1336, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "directional_offset_by", "qualified_name": "directional_offset_by", "args": ["self", "position_angle", "separation"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Computes coordinates at the given offset from this coordinate.\n\nParameters\n----------\nposition_angle : `~astropy.coordinates.Angle`\n    position_angle of offset\nseparation : `~astropy.coordinates.Angle`\n    offset angular separation\n\nReturns\n-------\nnewpoints : `~astropy.coordinates.SkyCoord`\n    Th", "start_line": 1338, "end_line": 1383, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "match_to_catalog_sky", "qualified_name": "match_to_catalog_sky", "args": ["self", "catalogcoord", "nthneighbor"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Finds the nearest on-sky matches of this coordinate in a set of\ncatalog coordinates.\n\nFor more on how to use this (and related) functionality, see the\nexamples in :doc:`astropy:/coordinates/matchsep`.\n\nParameters\n----------\ncatalogcoord : `~astropy.coordinates.SkyCoord` or `~astropy.coordinates.Base", "start_line": 1385, "end_line": 1448, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "match_to_catalog_3d", "qualified_name": "match_to_catalog_3d", "args": ["self", "catalogcoord", "nthneighbor"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Finds the nearest 3-dimensional matches of this coordinate to a set\nof catalog coordinates.\n\nThis finds the 3-dimensional closest neighbor, which is only different\nfrom the on-sky distance if ``distance`` is set in this object or the\n``catalogcoord`` object.\n\nFor more on how to use this (and related", "start_line": 1450, "end_line": 1516, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "search_around_sky", "qualified_name": "search_around_sky", "args": ["self", "searcharoundcoords", "seplimit"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Searches for all coordinates in this object around a supplied set of\npoints within a given on-sky separation.\n\nThis is intended for use on `~astropy.coordinates.SkyCoord` objects\nwith coordinate arrays, rather than a scalar coordinate.  For a scalar\ncoordinate, it is better to use\n`~astropy.coordina", "start_line": 1518, "end_line": 1576, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "search_around_3d", "qualified_name": "search_around_3d", "args": ["self", "searcharoundcoords", "distlimit"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Searches for all coordinates in this object around a supplied set of\npoints within a given 3D radius.\n\nThis is intended for use on `~astropy.coordinates.SkyCoord` objects\nwith coordinate arrays, rather than a scalar coordinate.  For a scalar\ncoordinate, it is better to use\n`~astropy.coordinates.SkyC", "start_line": 1578, "end_line": 1636, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "position_angle", "qualified_name": "position_angle", "args": ["self", "other"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Computes the on-sky position angle (East of North) between this\nSkyCoord and another.\n\nParameters\n----------\nother : |SkyCoord|\n    The other coordinate to compute the position angle to.  It is\n    treated as the \"head\" of the vector of the position angle.\n\nReturns\n-------\npa : `~astropy.coordinates", "start_line": 1638, "end_line": 1683, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "skyoffset_frame", "qualified_name": "skyoffset_frame", "args": ["self", "rotation"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Returns the sky offset frame with this SkyCoord at the origin.\n\nParameters\n----------\nrotation : angle-like\n    The final rotation of the frame about the ``origin``. The sign of\n    the rotation is the left-hand rule. That is, an object at a\n    particular position angle in the un-rotated system wil", "start_line": 1685, "end_line": 1706, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "get_constellation", "qualified_name": "get_constellation", "args": ["self", "short_name", "constellation_list"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Determines the constellation(s) of the coordinates this SkyCoord contains.\n\nParameters\n----------\nshort_name : bool\n    If True, the returned names are the IAU-sanctioned abbreviated\n    names.  Otherwise, full names for the constellations are used.\nconstellation_list : str\n    The set of constellat", "start_line": 1708, "end_line": 1748, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "to_pixel", "qualified_name": "to_pixel", "args": ["self", "wcs", "origin", "mode"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Convert this coordinate to pixel coordinates using a `~astropy.wcs.WCS`\nobject.\n\nParameters\n----------\nwcs : `~astropy.wcs.WCS`\n    The WCS to use for convert\norigin : int\n    Whether to return 0 or 1-based pixel coordinates.\nmode : 'all' or 'wcs'\n    Whether to do the transformation including disto", "start_line": 1754, "end_line": 1780, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "from_pixel", "qualified_name": "from_pixel", "args": ["cls", "xp", "yp", "wcs", "origin", "mode"], "decorators": ["classmethod"], "docstring": "Create a new SkyCoord from pixel coordinates using a World Coordinate System.\n\nParameters\n----------\nxp, yp : float or ndarray\n    The coordinates to convert.\nwcs : `~astropy.wcs.WCS`\n    The WCS to use for convert\norigin : int\n    Whether to return 0 or 1-based pixel coordinates.\nmode : 'all' or 'w", "start_line": 1783, "end_line": 1812, "is_async": false, "is_method": false}, {"name": "contained_by", "qualified_name": "contained_by", "args": ["self", "wcs", "image"], "decorators": [], "docstring": "Determines if the SkyCoord is contained in the given wcs f